WEBVTT

00:00.050 --> 00:03.080
We just looked at product rule and quotient rule for exponents.

00:03.110 --> 00:08.790
Now we want to talk about power rule for exponents, which we need when we have something like this.

00:08.810 --> 00:12.930
So here we have two exponents involved in the same expression.

00:12.950 --> 00:15.770
We have this first exponent, which is two.

00:15.800 --> 00:18.410
So we have three squared or three to the power of two.

00:18.530 --> 00:24.980
And then we're taking that whole thing and raising it to another exponent, this power of three here.

00:24.980 --> 00:32.060
So the way that we deal with this without a specific rule for exponents is just to realize that this

00:32.060 --> 00:39.620
three here, this outer exponent, tells us that we need to multiply together three factors of this

00:39.620 --> 00:40.340
base.

00:40.340 --> 00:43.210
And we're thinking about the whole base as three squared.

00:43.220 --> 00:49.550
So when we treat three squared as the entire base, this exponent of three tells us we need three factors

00:49.550 --> 00:50.120
of that.

00:50.120 --> 00:58.220
So we could rewrite this as three squared times three squared times three squared.

00:58.220 --> 01:01.260
And now these two expressions are equivalent.

01:01.290 --> 01:06.980
Now at this point, we can go back to the product rule for exponents that we learned earlier.

01:06.990 --> 01:14.310
What we see is that we have the same base where multiplying terms with exponents and each one has the

01:14.310 --> 01:15.360
same base.

01:15.360 --> 01:23.100
And the product rule for exponents tells us that when that's the case, we keep the base the same and

01:23.100 --> 01:24.330
we add the exponents.

01:24.330 --> 01:30.120
So this expression can be rewritten as three to the two plus two.

01:30.630 --> 01:32.430
Plus two or.

01:33.380 --> 01:34.550
Three to the six.

01:34.580 --> 01:38.070
Now, here's where power rule comes in.

01:38.090 --> 01:44.480
It gives us a shorthand way of expressing three squared and then that whole thing raised to the power

01:44.480 --> 01:46.350
of three as three to the sixth.

01:46.370 --> 01:52.520
What it tells us is that in order to get from this expression to the simplified version three to the

01:52.520 --> 01:56.150
sixth, all we have to do is multiply the exponents.

01:56.150 --> 02:01.910
So instead of writing the expanded product this way and then applying the product rule for exponents,

02:01.910 --> 02:07.970
instead, we can apply power rule and recognize that to simplify this, all we have to do is multiply

02:07.970 --> 02:12.580
the two exponents, two and three to get a new exponent of six.

02:12.590 --> 02:18.380
So when we have any a raised to the power of M, and then we take that whole thing and we raise it to

02:18.380 --> 02:23.480
the power of N, we know that that's equal to a to the M times.

02:23.480 --> 02:28.630
N In other words, keep the base the same and just multiply the exponents.

02:28.640 --> 02:34.350
So if we look at another example and this time, instead of expanding it, this way, or in fact, let's

02:34.350 --> 02:39.510
just do it both ways so we can see that the power rule works if we go back to this way that we did it

02:39.510 --> 02:40.350
originally.

02:40.350 --> 02:46.680
This exponent of four tells us that we need to multiply together four factors of two squared the base.

02:46.680 --> 02:48.810
So we would say two squared.

02:49.230 --> 02:50.820
And we would ride out.

02:51.320 --> 02:52.610
Four of these.

02:52.850 --> 03:00.560
And then we would apply the product rule for exponents, which tells us to keep the base the same and

03:00.560 --> 03:01.880
add the exponents.

03:01.880 --> 03:09.110
So we'd have two plus two plus two plus 2 or 2 to the eighth power.

03:09.110 --> 03:14.480
But of course, as we can see, the power rule just lets us do that In one step.

03:14.480 --> 03:16.550
We multiply the exponents of two and four.

03:16.550 --> 03:17.960
We know two times four is eight.

03:17.960 --> 03:23.840
So this just becomes two to the eight and we save ourselves all these steps.

03:23.840 --> 03:25.250
Let's do one more.

03:25.280 --> 03:27.740
This time we have three to the power of two.

03:27.740 --> 03:29.030
This time we're squaring it.

03:29.060 --> 03:36.110
We should know right away from the power rule for exponents that that is three to the fourth power.

03:36.110 --> 03:41.510
But if we did it the other way, this exponent of two tells us to multiply together two factors of this

03:41.510 --> 03:42.710
base three squared.

03:42.710 --> 03:46.730
So we have three squared multiplied by three squared.

03:46.760 --> 03:51.930
We have two factors multiplied together of three squared and then product rule for exponents tells us

03:51.930 --> 03:54.660
to keep the base the same and add the exponents.

03:54.660 --> 03:58.350
So three to the two plus two power or.

03:58.930 --> 04:01.690
Three to the four and we get the same answer.

04:01.690 --> 04:07.390
So that's the power rule for exponents, a really helpful exponent rule that we can add to our library

04:07.390 --> 04:11.770
of exponent rules along with product rule and quotient rule.
